<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the web.config the SDK generates at publish. Everything the SDK writes — the
  aspNetCore handler, the process path, the hosting model — is added to this file rather than
  replaced by it, so nothing here needs to repeat it.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: IIS refuses a query string longer than 2048 characters with 404.15,
  BEFORE the request reaches the application. Nothing in the app logs it, and what comes back is
  an IIS error page, so it reads as "the URL is wrong" rather than "the URL is too long".

  Media tickets go over that. /media/{id}/{kind}?t=... carries the viewer's API access token
  encrypted with Data Protection, and a real one measured 2504 characters — comfortably past the
  limit, and only just, which is why it looked intermittent: whether a page's images worked
  depended on how long that viewer's token happened to be. Found 2026-08-27, when Ben's profile
  photos would not display on ishaunted.com while working perfectly on localhost, because Kestrel
  has no such limit and IIS does.

  16384 is chosen to be far enough above the current 2504 that ordinary growth in the token does
  not bring this back, while staying well inside what IIS accepts. It is a ceiling on a URL, not
  on a request body — uploads are governed by maxAllowedContentLength and by the app's own
  configurable limit, neither of which this touches.

  This is the immediate half of the fix. The durable half is to stop putting a whole access token
  in a URL at all — see the backlog item — because a limit raised is still a limit, and the next
  thing to grow past it will fail the same silent way.
-->
<configuration>
  <location path="." inheritInChildApplications="false">
    <system.webServer>
      <security>
        <requestFiltering>
          <requestLimits maxQueryString="16384" maxUrl="16384" />
        </requestFiltering>
      </security>
    </system.webServer>
  </location>
</configuration>
